  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front is continuing to pass through the \nregion today.\n\nA cold front is currently moving through the region. As of this\nafternoon, the front is located near portions of New Jersey and\nDelmarva. Sufficient CAPE and supportive bulk shear values have\nallowed convection to blossom this afternoon across portions of\nNew Jersey and Delmarva. There is the potential for an isolated\nsevere thunderstorm with a Marginal (level 1 out of 5) risk for\nDelmarva and southern New Jersey this afternoon into the early\nevening. The PWAT values also remain generally around \n1.50-1.75\" with warm cloud layer processes supporting showers \nand storms producing efficient rainfall leading to localized\nflash flooding. For areas in Delmarva and southern NJ, there is\na Marginal (level 1 out of 4) risk for excessive rainfall.\n\nBehind the cold front, it looks drier into at least tomorrow. A\nhigh pressure system builds in for Saturday keeping the area \ndry. An upper-level disturbance moving through the region on \nSunday has the potential to result in some showers and \nthunderstorms, particularly during the afternoon and evening\nhours.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Increasing heat and humidity return next week \nwith unsettled weather.\n\nBroad upper level ridging returns to the eastern US early next week, \nbringing in surface high pressure across the eastern US.\nAfternoon highs will creep back into the upper 80s to low 90s, about \n5 degrees above average for early September. Dew points will also \ncreep back into the low 70s. One other aspect to watch with this\nridging pattern is the potential for thunderstorms to rotate \naround the periphery of the ridge and the position of a warm\nfront next week. The exact location, timing, and intensity of \nthe thunderstorms remains a bit uncertain at this range. \nCurrently, the main days to monitor are Tuesday and Wednesday \nregarding heavy rain and severe weather potential.",
